import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class ExecutorExample {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2); // 2 threads in pool

        executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("Task 1 by " + Thread.currentThread().getName()));
        executor.submit(() -> System.out.println("Task 2 by " + Thread.currentThread().getName()));  /// here it will execute thread if you want result in return the you would use callable and future


ExecutorService executor = Executors.newSingleThreadExecutor(); // here it will execute thread if you want result in return the you would use callable and future
Callable<String> task = () -> "Task Completed!";
Future<String> result = executor.submit(task);


        executor.shutdown(); // Always shutdown after use
    }
}


🔹 Common Factory Methods (from Executors class):
| Method                      | Description                             |
| --------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| `newFixedThreadPool(int n)` | Fixed number of threads                 |
| `newCachedThreadPool()`     | Creates new threads as needed           |
| `newSingleThreadExecutor()` | Single thread executor                  |
| `newScheduledThreadPool(n)` | Run tasks after a delay or periodically |


🔸 Important Methods in ExecutorService:
| Method               | Description                                   |
| -------------------- | --------------------------------------------- |
| `submit(Runnable)`   | Submits a task for execution                  |
| `shutdown()`         | Initiates orderly shutdown                    |
| `shutdownNow()`      | Attempts to stop all actively executing tasks |
| `awaitTermination()` | Waits for termination of all tasks            |

